title The follow-up that left the nib behind
prompt the same lap, continued through its send
think The work commit 334cc1f4b landed with the card's Git nib naming 38ce7eb0a1, its own parent, which is what the rule asks.
think Closing the fleet claim then put a second commit on top, and that commit carried real content -- the board record removed -- so it is a work commit rather than a pin-only one.
think It still left the nib two commits back, and remember_git_nib went red: the nib names neither HEAD, nor HEAD's pre-amend sibling, nor HEAD's parent.
think Rule 5 of remember-git-nib says a follow-up carries the nib forward, rewritten to HEAD read before the follow-up is made. I read the rule and did not apply it to the claim close.
obs The repair cannot be a pin-only commit, which the same rule forbids, so the nib rides beside this log -- the record of the send, written after the send, which is the one thing that could not have gone in the first commit.
obs The lesson is narrow and worth keeping: a claim close is a follow-up like any other, and the nib carry is owed by every commit that lands on top of the work commit, not only by a log.
rota none -- the rota was read on this lap's first half, row 3, and this half is the send
file construction/ITINERARY.md the Git nib carried to ae27b4d817, HEAD read before this commit
